 "dualwrite": {
  "ci/check_skill_deploy_drift.py": {
   "bytes": 12525,
   "head": [
    "\"\"\"L10 skill-deploy drift reporter -- the missing vault->live half of the sync architecture.",
    "",
    "WHY THIS EXISTS",
    "---------------",
    "The repo already ships three sync scripts and NONE of them can answer \"is the",
    "LOADED skill set stale?\":",
    "",
    "  * ``scripts/sync-skills.ps1``   repo ``skills/``          -> ``~/.agents/skills``",
    "  * ``scripts/sync-from-live.ps1`` ``~/.agents/skills``     -> repo ``.agents/skills``",
    "  * ``ci/check_skill_dual_write.py`` repo ``skills/``  <->  repo ``.agents/skills``",
    "",
    "``~/.claude/skills`` is a SYMLINK to ``~/.claude/skills.daily``, and that curated",
    "subset is what Claude actually loads each session.  MEASURED 2026-07-29: not one",
    "script in ``scripts/`` or ``ci/`` writes to ``skills.daily`` (probe:",
    "``Select-String -Path scripts/* ci/* -Pattern 'skills\\\\.daily' -SimpleMatch`` ->",
    "0 hits).  The deploy step into the loaded tree is performed BY HAND, which is why",
    "it drifts.",
    "",
    "WHY IT REPORTS AND REFUSES TO COPY",
    "----------------------------------",
    "The obvious \"fix\" -- copy the vault over the live tree -- is DESTRUCTIVE here.",
    "Measured 2026-07-29 across the 79 shared skills of the live tree: 37 identical,",
    "**0 where the vault is a strict superset**, 8 live-ahead and 34 diverged.  A",
    "blind vault->live sync would therefore have destroyed live-unique content in 42",
    "of 79 skills.  Re-measured 2026-07-30 with a second, independent algorithm",
    "(``difflib.ndiff`` LCS opcodes rather than set membership): 42/0/4/33 -- the",
    "**0 vault-supersets result is stable under both**, which is the load-bearing",
    "number; the other three drifted within a day, so treat any count here as a",
    "dated sample and re-run the tool rather than quoting it.",
    "",
    "Both copies hold substantive unique lines: the divergence is two-directional,",
    "so no one-directional sync is provably safe.  (An earlier version of this",
    "docstring illustrated that with three specific lines from",
    "``codex-databricks-fanout/SKILL.md`` -- a ``## 9. Harness paths`` section, a"
   ]
  },
  "ci/tests/test_check_skill_dual_write.py": {
   "bytes": 27873,
   "head": [
    "\"\"\"Acceptance tests for the D-001 dual-write guard.",
    "",
    "Every behavioural assertion drives the guard as a REAL SUBPROCESS and reads",
    "``CompletedProcess.returncode`` directly.  Nothing is piped: a pipeline replaces",
    "the guard's own exit status with the last stage's, which is exactly how an",
    "earlier verification pass printed ``EXIT=0`` next to output that read",
    "``D-001 DUAL-WRITE VIOLATION``.",
    "",
    "Naming: ``test_control_*`` are the both-states controls (a detector that is",
    "silent in both states measures nothing); the defect tests carry the defect id",
    "from the lane's remediation matrix.",
    "\"\"\"",
    "",
    "from __future__ import annotations",
    "",
    "import json",
    "import os",
    "import subprocess",
    "import sys",
    "from pathlib import Path",
    "",
    "from dual_write_fixtures import Repo",
    "",
    "GUARD = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / \"check_skill_dual_write.py\"",
    "",
    "NEW_SKILL = \"# probe-skill\\n\\nreal content\\n\"",
    "EDIT = \"# existing-skill\\n\\nedited content\\n\"",
    "",
    "",
    "def run_guard(repo: Repo, *args: str) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess[str]:",
    "    return subprocess.run(",
    "        [sys.executable, str(GUARD), \"--repo\", str(repo.root), *args],",
    "        capture_output=True,",
    "        text=True,"
   ]
  },
  "tools/d001-dual-write/README.md": {
   "bytes": 8663,
   "head": [
    "# `d001_apply.py` \u2014 filter-safe D-001 dual-write applier",
    "",
    "`.agents/skills/` is the authoritative skill root; `skills/` is a compatibility",
    "projection (`AGENTS.md`). When a change lands a skill under `skills/<NAME>/`",
    "only, the fix is to mirror the **same blobs** onto `.agents/skills/<NAME>/`.",
    "",
    "This tool does that with **plumbing only**. It never creates a second working",
    "tree, never restores the whole index, and never round-trips a file through a",
    "content filter. Protected paths are therefore never opened for write: their",
    "bytes *and* their mtime are unchanged by a full run.",
    "",
    "## Why the obvious procedure is unsafe here",
    "",
    "This repository encrypts `tools/mcp/*/accounts.json` with git-crypt",
    "(`.gitattributes`). Every materialising git operation writes files through that",
    "filter's *smudge* side. If the filter has been reduced to an identity",
    "passthrough \u2014 `smudge = cat`, `clean = cat`, `required = false` in the **shared**",
    "`.git/config`, a state this repository was measurably in \u2014 then:",
    "",
    "1. **The damage lands on the first command.** A second working tree of the same",
    "   repository inherits the shared config, so creating one writes the STORED",
    "   (encrypted) bytes into it for every protected path, including paths the",
    "   procedure never intended to touch. Narrowing a *later* per-file step does not",
    "   help: the tree is already corrupt before that step is reached.",
    "2. **`git status` cannot detect it.** With `clean = cat`, the stored bytes map",
    "   back to the identical blob, so `git status --porcelain` prints nothing over a",
    "   working tree full of ciphertext. Any safety check built on `git status`",
    "   measures nothing during exactly the window in which the damage occurs.",
    "3. **The blow-up is deferred.** Restoring the real filter makes the file show as",
    "   modified \u2014 and committing it then stores a *re-encrypted* blob, different from",
    "   the one on record.",
    "",
    "`preflight` is built for (2) and (3): it inspects CONTENT with two mechanically",
    "independent signals \u2014 a stored-form magic prefix read from the file, and"
   ]
  }
 },
